# TextFSM template: Cisco IOS / IOS-XE
# `show bgp vrf <VRF> {ipv4|ipv6} unicast summary`.
#
# Sample output (truncated):
#   BGP router identifier 10.0.0.1, local AS number 65000
#   BGP table version is 100, main routing table version 100
#   100 network entries using 12345 bytes of memory
#   ...
#   Neighbor        V           AS MsgRcvd MsgSent   TblVer  InQ OutQ Up/Down  State/PfxRcd
#   10.0.0.2        4        65001     123     124      100    0    0 1d02h        100
#   10.0.0.3        4        65002       0       0        1    0    0 never        Idle
#   2001:db8::2     4        65003     234     235      100    0    0 00:01:00 OpenSent
#
# On established sessions the last column is the prefix count
# (numeric); on non-Established sessions it carries the state name
# (`Idle`, `Active`, `OpenSent`, `OpenConfirm`, `Idle (Admin)`).
# We capture the column once into state_or_pfx and let the projector
# decide based on whether it parses as a number.
#
# IOS does not report PfxSnt in the standard `summary` output —
# the operator has to run `show bgp ... neighbors <peer> | i prefix`
# for that. We leave prefixes_sent zero rather than guess.
Value Filldown local_asn (\d+)
Value Filldown router_id ([0-9a-fA-F:.]+)
Value Required peer_ip ([0-9a-fA-F:.]+)
Value peer_asn (\d+)
Value uptime (\S+)
Value state (\S+(?:\s+\(\w+\))?)
Value prefixes_received (\d+)

Start
  ^BGP\s+router\s+identifier\s+${router_id},\s+local\s+AS\s+number\s+${local_asn}
  ^Neighbor\s+V\s+AS\s+MsgRcvd\s+MsgSent.*$$ -> Peers

Peers
  # Established session: trailing numeric column = prefixes_received.
  # We synthesise the state="Established" in the projector when
  # prefixes_received is present and state is empty.
  ^\s*${peer_ip}\s+\d+\s+${peer_asn}\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+${uptime}\s+${prefixes_received}\s*$$ -> Record
  # Non-established session: trailing column = state keyword.
  ^\s*${peer_ip}\s+\d+\s+${peer_asn}\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+${uptime}\s+${state}\s*$$ -> Record
  ^\s*$$ -> Start
